{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Behind the way of the going down of the sun, etc.</b> All these signs and specifications appear to contain no great necessity, for these mountains that were designated by name were recognizable and well known. And in tractate Sotah (33b) it concludes: 'That dwell in the Arabah' — but did they not dwell among mountains and hills? 'Over against Gilgal' — but had they not seen Gilgal? Rabbi Elazar ben Jacob says: the verse came only to show them the way on the second occasion just as He showed them on the first: 'the way' — on the road, go, and not through fields and vineyards; 'that dwell' — in settled areas, go, and not through wildernesses; 'in the Arabah' — on level ground, go, and not through mountains and hills. And according to his words it is difficult: what does this have to do with Mount Gerizim and Mount Ebal? And by way of allusion one may say that He indicated that the blessings would come upon three classes of Israel when they walk a straight path." "The first class — they are the perfect of the way, who walk in the Torah of HaShem, the instructors of the law in Israel. To them He said: on the road, go, and not through fields and vineyards, just as the priests had no portion in fields and vineyards, so that they would not be preoccupied with working the ground, but rather 'the lips of a priest shall guard knowledge, and they shall seek Torah from his mouth' (Malachi 2:7). And we find that the whole Torah is called 'a way,' as is stated in Kiddushin (2a); so too all who walk the way of the Torah of HaShem shall not engage in fields and vineyards." "The second class — they are the householders who engage in the settlement of the world and support the hands of those who learn Torah. To them He said: in settled areas, go — that you shall always be occupied with matters that are the settlement of the world and not with idle things, matters of void like the wilderness, a place of void. This is what it says: and not through wildernesses, like the way of dwellers in corners, whose entire trade and commerce is in idle things and evil speech — and this would include the expression 'wildernesses.'" "The third class — they are the holders of authority, the kings of Israel and the princes of every generation, and their leaders. To them He said that their hearts should not be lifted up, but 'in the Arabah they shall go' — on level ground and not through mountains and hills, to walk in greatnesses and wonders, but rather on the middle path, just as the Arabah is intermediate between mountain and lowland. And even though this entire verse speaks of actual wayfarers — for the verse does not depart from its plain sense — and as Rashi explained, that He commanded them to go by way of the Canaanite, for it is a land of level ground and settlement, just as He showed them on the first occasion; nevertheless it was not for nothing that Moses went back and urged them a second time about the conduct of the way, but rather so that from the plain meaning of this way they might learn the conduct of the way of HaShem in Torah and commandment, through which they will merit the blessings spoken on Mount Gerizim in this world, and for the reward of the world to come that comes 'by the way of the going down of the sun' — after the setting of a man's sun in this world, he shines there in eternal light. And even though all this is the way of derash, nevertheless the matter in and of itself is sound and close to hearing; let a wise man hear and add learning."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The citation מסכת סוטה (לג:) resolved as 33b based on the standard pagination where this sugya appears."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:12:20.441028188Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}